---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const updated = 'September 2026';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How to Reprice Recurring Cleaning Clients (Without Losing Them) — Main Street Toolbox"
  description="A step-by-step system for repricing recurring cleaning accounts: track real hours per client, find the creep, announce the increase with 30 days notice, tier the rise, and use grandfather locks to keep price-sensitive clients."
  path="/guides/reprice-recurring-clients/"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Operations guide · {updated}</p>
    <h1>How to reprice recurring cleaning clients</h1>
    <p><b>The short answer:</b> track actual hours per account, compare against your target hourly, announce
    increases 30 days out with a specific date, tier the rise (underpriced accounts take more, fair ones take
    less), and offer a grandfather lock for price-sensitive clients. Most owners who "feel" an account is bad
    are right — but two quarters late, because creep is gradual.</p>

    <h2>Step 1: catch the creep with three columns</h2>
    <p>On your schedule sheet, keep three numbers per recurring account: <b>monthly price, actual hours worked
    (written down at the end of each visit, not estimated), and price ÷ real hours</b>. At month end, any
    account below your target hourly goes on the repricing list.</p>
    <p>For solo residential work, most cleaners need <b>$45–60/hour loaded</b> to actually survive — wage,
    taxes, insurance, supplies and drive time all come out of that number. An account that pencils at $28/hour
    isn't a client; it's a subscription you're paying for.</p>

    <h2>Step 2: know the three ways good accounts go bad</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>How it happens</th><th>The tell</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Labor time drift</td><td>Same rooms, more minutes, every visit — nobody notices 8 extra minutes a week</td><td>Price ÷ real hours has slid over 2–3 months</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scope creep</td><td>"While you're here..." requests that never come with a price</td><td>The task list on the quote no longer matches reality</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drive time</td><td>20 minutes each way eats a full hour of weekly capacity</td><td>Two accounts, same price, one takes 5 hrs the other 9</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Callbacks and re-cleans are the hidden column almost nobody tracks — one free re-do can erase a month
    of margin on a small account. Log them like any other visit. And the uncomfortable one: <b>your oldest
    accounts are usually your worst</b>, precisely because loyalty makes you rationalize what the hours math
    would flag immediately.</p>

    <h2>Step 3: the reprice process</h2>
    <p>A reprice is a <b>process, not a conversation</b>. The structure that works:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Announce a pricing review</b> — not a surprise. New rate effective next quarter.</li>
      <li><b>30 days written notice</b> with the specific date. Specific dates get paid faster and argued less than "next month".</li>
      <li><b>The email says four things:</b> why (real costs, specifically), what (the new rate), when (the exact date), and what-if (one contact for questions).</li>
      <li><b>Tier the increase.</b> If you're repricing at scale, don't do a flat 10%: the accounts you've been underpricing for a year take 12–15%, the fairly-priced ones take 4–5%, the overpriced ones take nothing. Same total revenue impact, half the churn risk.</li>
      <li><b>If the gap is over ~20%, unbundle instead.</b> Keep the base price, put the extras (interior windows, fridge, pet hair) on a priced add-on list and let the client choose what their budget buys. You'll be surprised how many pick the add-ons — which tells you the account was never the problem, the scope was.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Step 4: the grandfather lock</h2>
    <p>The lever almost nobody uses: offer to <b>lock the current rate for 12 months in exchange for an annual
    commitment</b> (paid upfront or on contract). A meaningful chunk of your price-sensitive clients take it —
    which is upfront cash for you and guaranteed retention — while the rest move to the new rate without a
    fight.</p>
    <p>Expect the cancellation calls in the two weeks after notices go out, not on the effective date. Have one
    scripted concession ready (midpoint rate, or the old rate for quarterly prepay) — most callers just want to
    feel heard once.</p>

    <h2>Step 5: raise more than you think you need</h2>
    <p>Going back for another 5% next year hurts twice. Operators who price in <b>8–10% annual steps train
    their market</b>; the ones who do 3% train their clients to expect a fight every time. The increase funds
    the next year's wage rise — if you skip it, you're the one paying it.</p>

    <h2>Run the numbers</h2>
    <p>Two tools carry the math: the <a href="/restaurant/labor-cost-calculator/">Labor Cost Percentage
    Calculator</a> shows what your target hourly actually needs to be (fully loaded with taxes and benefits),
    and the <a href="/cleaning/cleaning-estimate-calculator/">Cleaning Estimate Calculator</a> prices new
    accounts at the correct band from day one — so the repricing list stays short. Every benchmark band on this
    page is also on the <a href="/benchmarks/service-pricing-2026/">2026 Service Business Pricing Benchmarks</a>
    page with sources.</p>

    <h2>Questions owners ask</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>How often should I review recurring account profitability?</summary><p>Monthly on the hours column (it's three numbers, five minutes), and a full re-time of every recurring account twice a year. Scope creep is invisible month to month — the six-month mark is where it shows up in the math.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How much notice before a price increase?</summary><p>30 days minimum for residential, 60 for commercial contracts. The notice must contain the specific effective date — "prices going up next month" creates arguments, "your new rate begins March 1" creates calendar entries.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What's a reasonable annual increase?</summary><p>8–10% per year is the band that keeps pace with wage growth without re-arguing every year. Under 5% reads as negotiable; over 15% on a fair-priced account reads as greedy. Tier it — underpriced accounts can take 15%+ precisely because they were wrong to begin with.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Should I ever fire a client instead of repricing?</summary><p>Yes, sometimes. If the account needs more than a 20% correction AND the client is difficult, the polite version of firing is the unbundle offer — they either accept a properly scoped price or self-select out. Don't keep an account that costs you money out of loyalty; that money is coming from your family, not from nostalgia.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
